// Content is shared by presenter and study modes. Cases are original, fictional teaching examples.
export const modules = [
{
id:1,act:1,title:'The new reality of educational AI leadership',short:'A leadership challenge',space:'The classroom',question:'Why is AI implementation a leadership challenge rather than simply a technology challenge?',
lead:'The same technology can enter two schools and create two very different experiences. The difference begins with the decisions people make around it.',
definition:'Educational AI leadership is the work of aligning AI use with learning purposes, professional expertise, shared values, and accountable organizational practices.',
analysis:[
'Adoption means that a tool is available or used. Transformation means that teaching practices and learning opportunities improve in ways the community can explain and evaluate. A license count measures access, not understanding. AI can draft lessons, comments, messages, and summaries, but each output still needs a qualified person to check disciplinary accuracy, context, and suitability.',
'This changes professional responsibilities as well as workflow. A teacher must know when an output is unreliable; a principal must establish who reviews a vendor; a district must decide which data may be entered. Without those structures, even a useful tool can create inconsistent expectations, hidden workload, or unequal access. Institutional readiness includes time, support, infrastructure, and the capacity to stop an unsuitable practice.',
'Leaders therefore start with a student need, listen to educators, and define boundaries before encouraging experimentation. Shared values turn “use AI” into a more defensible aim, such as improving timely feedback while preserving teacher judgment. The leadership challenge is to create conditions in which educators can question, adapt, and evaluate the tool rather than accept its outputs as authoritative.'
],
elementary:'A grade 3 team uses a tool to draft differentiated reading questions. The leader provides common planning time and requires teachers to check reading demands, factual accuracy, and relevance before use. Students explain their reasoning; the team reviews whether the questions support comprehension.',
secondary:'A high school department pilots AI-generated feedback on practice essays. Teachers compare comments with a common rubric, check examples of misleading feedback, and retain responsibility for final judgments. A student can challenge a comment and receive a human explanation.',
inclusion:'Include special educators and multilingual specialists in the initial review. A simplified passage may remove important meaning; translation may flatten cultural context. Provide accessible formats and an equivalent learning route for students who cannot or should not use the tool.',
limits:'A careful pilot does not guarantee success. Staff may still have too little time, and a tool may not serve the identified need. Leadership must avoid both uncritical enthusiasm and the assumption that every use is harmful.',
strategies:['Name one instructional problem and a non-AI comparison.','Map who approves tools, checks outputs, supports educators, and hears concerns.','Pilot with explicit boundaries, an accessible alternative, and a date for review.'],
reflection:'Where in your organization is technology use being mistaken for educational improvement? What evidence would distinguish the two?',
synthesis:'A tool creates an option. Leadership determines the conditions under which that option becomes educationally worthwhile.',sources:['brief','ed'],activity:'schools',model:['Learning need','Educator judgment','Shared safeguards','Evidence of learning']
},
{
id:2,act:1,title:'Developing a strategic vision',short:'Start with the learning',space:'The leadership planning office',question:'What should AI implementation accomplish?',
lead:'A compelling vision names the learning we want to make possible—and the evidence that would show we are getting there.',
definition:'A strategic vision is a shared, feasible description of a desired educational future that directs priorities, resources, and decisions.',
analysis:[
'A technology-centered goal asks how many people will use AI. A learning-centered goal asks which barriers learners face and how practice could change. “Every teacher uses a chatbot” is an activity target; “students receive feedback they can explain and use to revise” describes an educational purpose. Vision development connects the school mission, community values, student needs, and existing initiatives.',
'A logic model separates inputs (time, staff, licenses), activities (coaching and pilots), outputs (teachers trained or materials produced), changes in practice (verified, actionable feedback), and student outcomes (stronger independent explanations or revisions). Each link is a hypothesis, not an automatic chain of benefits. A plan should say what might break the link and who will investigate.',
'Consultation makes the vision more specific and feasible. Students may value understandable feedback; families may prioritize privacy; educators may need protected collaboration time. A leader reconciles those priorities through a limited scope, a resource plan, baseline evidence, and a review date. Measurable objectives should include quality and distribution of benefits, not only averages. A coherent vision also explains which competing projects will wait.'
],
elementary:'A school prioritizes reading comprehension over automated worksheet production. Its pilot supports teachers in drafting accessible discussion questions, then compares students’ explanations using an agreed rubric and checks access for each learner group.',
secondary:'A department wants more useful feedback on evidence-based writing. It allocates coaching time, samples revisions before and after the pilot, and asks students to identify which feedback they used. It tracks teacher review time rather than assuming automation saves time.',
inclusion:'Invite learners who use assistive technology and multilingual families into goal setting. Disaggregate access and experience where privacy permits. A favorable overall result can conceal a barrier for a smaller group; protect small-group confidentiality.',
limits:'Targets can distort practice when they become quotas. Baselines and small pilots can suggest a direction but cannot establish causal effects. A vision must be revised if staff capacity or student experience contradicts its assumptions.',
strategies:['Write a goal that describes a change in learning, not a purchasing decision.','Attach an owner, baseline, indicator, review date, and equity check.','Identify an activity to discontinue so the plan fits real staff time.'],
reflection:'Rewrite an AI adoption goal as a learning goal. What would you observe in students’ work if it succeeded?',
synthesis:'A strong vision connects purpose to resources, practice, and evidence while keeping those categories distinct.',sources:['brief'],activity:'vision',model:['Inputs','Activities','Outputs','Practice','Outcomes']
},
{
id:3,act:1,title:'Leading people through change',short:'Listen before you launch',space:'The faculty collaboration room',question:'How do leaders guide educators through technological change?',
lead:'A concern voiced in a faculty meeting may be the most useful implementation evidence you have.',
definition:'Change leadership is the relational and strategic work of helping people understand, shape, and sustain a change in professional practice.',
analysis:[
'Educators encounter change through workload, professional identity, autonomy, and trust—not simply through technical readiness. A teacher concerned about hallucinations may be defending instructional quality. A special educator asking for accessibility testing may be identifying a genuine barrier. Treating all resistance as unwillingness suppresses information that a sound implementation needs.',
'Supportive leadership invites participation in a clear direction. A representative coalition can define the problem, surface constraints, model bounded practice, and communicate what is still uncertain. Listening does not mean that every preference decides policy; it means that decisions reflect concerns and explain the reasons for tradeoffs. Transparency includes acknowledging where leaders lack evidence.',
'Incremental implementation offers space for coaching, early learning, and revision. Early successes should be specific examples of improved practice, not promotional stories stripped of limitations. Feedback channels need named owners and visible responses. Effective practices become sustainable when time, induction, policy, and budget support them; a temporary champion alone cannot institutionalize a change.'
],
elementary:'A teacher worries that AI lesson preparation will add another task. The principal asks the team to log drafting, checking, and adaptation time during a short pilot, then reduces a competing reporting requirement to create space for learning.',
secondary:'A veteran science teacher fears losing professional autonomy. The department invites teachers to design an approved-use pilot and retains teacher discretion over whether an AI suggestion fits the laboratory context. Safety and assessment requirements remain common.',
inclusion:'Provide multiple ways to participate: accessible written feedback, small-group conversation, and translation where needed. Do not equate fluent public speaking or technical confidence with expertise; specialists and support staff often notice overlooked implementation risks.',
limits:'Consultation without influence becomes performative. On the other hand, unlimited deliberation can prevent decisions. Communicate what is open to negotiation, what is required for safety, and when a decision will be made.',
strategies:['Ask what a concern reveals about conditions for success.','Build a coalition with varied roles and readiness levels.','Publish a short decision log: concern, response, unresolved question, next review.'],
reflection:'Which colleague’s hesitation could help you design a better implementation? How would you invite that expertise?',
synthesis:'Trust grows when leaders listen, explain boundaries, support practice, and show how feedback changes decisions.',sources:['brief'],activity:'faculty',model:['Listen','Co-design','Support','Review','Sustain']
},
{
id:4,act:1,title:'Building a culture for responsible innovation',short:'Make uncertainty speakable',space:'The collaboration network',question:'What organizational conditions support responsible innovation?',
lead:'Policy describes what should happen. Culture shapes what people feel able to do when the policy meets real life.',
definition:'Organizational culture is the pattern of shared beliefs, norms, expectations, and informal practices that shapes how people act together.',
analysis:[
'A staff handbook may require output verification while informal norms reward rapid production. In that environment, educators may hide uncertainty or quietly skip review. Psychological safety means people can ask questions and report mistakes without humiliation; it does not mean removing professional standards or accountability. The relevant question is whether concerns become shared learning or private risk.',
'Professional trust combines autonomy with clear responsibilities. Teachers need room to adapt a tool to their learners, but they also need common privacy boundaries and a way to document problems. Collaborative inquiry makes experimentation public enough to evaluate: a team explains its aim, examines student work, and discusses what did not work. A failed trial can be useful if it prevents wider harm.',
'Leadership practices travel through networks. When a principal models uncertainty, a coach shares a corrected output, and a specialist can challenge a design, educators learn that responsible use includes questioning. When status depends on appearing innovative, the same network can normalize shortcuts. Culture is built through repeated decisions about time, recognition, participation, and response to incidents.'
],
elementary:'A teaching team brings one AI-generated question that confused students to a planning meeting. Colleagues examine the misconception without blaming the teacher, then agree on a review routine and share a corrected example.',
secondary:'A school creates a cross-department inquiry group. Teachers keep autonomy over lesson design while sharing privacy incidents and assessment dilemmas through a common reporting process. Leaders publicly follow up on issues.',
inclusion:'Make specialists, multilingual educators, and paraprofessionals visible partners rather than last-stage reviewers. Ensure meetings, documents, and reporting routes are accessible. Culture is exclusionary if only confident early adopters can influence decisions.',
limits:'An invitation to experiment is unsafe without limits. Trust also requires appropriate action after repeated unsafe conduct. Avoid presenting psychological safety as permission to ignore student protections.',
strategies:['Model a specific uncertainty and explain how you checked it.','Recognize careful non-use and corrected errors alongside successful experiments.','Protect regular collaborative review time and respond visibly to reports.'],
reflection:'What happens in your setting when an educator admits an AI output was wrong? What does that response teach the organization?',
synthesis:'A responsible culture makes inquiry safe and accountability clear.',sources:['brief'],activity:'culture',model:['Leadership','Teachers','Specialists','Students','Families']
},
{
id:5,act:2,title:'Designing professional learning',short:'Capacity grows through practice',space:'The professional learning center',question:'How do leaders build educator capacity for responsible AI use?',
lead:'A workshop can introduce a tool. Sustained learning changes how an educator notices, judges, and acts.',
definition:'Professional learning is ongoing, practice-connected development through which educators strengthen knowledge, judgment, and instructional skill.',
analysis:[
'One-time demonstrations often privilege features and speed over evaluation. Responsible capacity includes understanding AI limitations, designing prompts, checking outputs, recognizing hallucinations, protecting privacy, and choosing appropriate non-use. Educators also need to connect these skills to accessibility, instructional design, feedback, and assessment. The aim is better professional decisions, not uniform enthusiasm.',
'Different roles require different pathways. A novice might compare an output with a trusted curriculum source; an experienced teacher might redesign a feedback routine; a leader might review procurement responsibilities. Coaching cycles connect a goal, modeling, supported practice, observation, and reflection. Professional learning communities can examine a common task and compare how learners respond.',
'A feasible plan acknowledges time and competing priorities. It provides a foundation before classroom use, repeated practice with de-identified examples, a coach or peer partner, and a follow-up review. Evaluate transfer by examining revised lesson materials, output-checking routines, learner experience, and teacher review time. Attendance and satisfaction are useful logistics, but neither proves instructional improvement.'
],
elementary:'Teachers first identify errors in a sample reading passage, then co-design questions with a literacy coach. They teach an approved lesson, collect anonymous examples of student explanations, and meet to revise the questions.',
secondary:'A department pairs prompt-design practice with assessment redesign. Colleagues compare an AI-written model essay with a rubric, develop explicit assistance boundaries, and rehearse student conferences before launching the task.',
inclusion:'Offer accessible learning materials, protected time, varied entry points, and specialist co-facilitation. Practice evaluating translation, screen-reader output, and accommodations without uploading real student records. Do not make educators pay personally for tools.',
limits:'Even strong learning cannot repair an unsuitable product. Coaching needs staffing and time. A short sequence should name what remains unlearned rather than suggest that attendance confers expertise.',
strategies:['Start with literacy, verification, and privacy before classroom application.','Differentiate by role and readiness while keeping shared safeguards.','Connect each session to an artifact, supported practice, and follow-up evidence.'],
reflection:'What would you remove from the calendar to make a meaningful coaching cycle possible?',
synthesis:'Capacity develops through repeated, supported judgment in real instructional work.',sources:['brief','learning'],activity:'learning',model:['Foundation','Model','Practice','Coach','Transfer']
},
{
id:6,act:2,title:'Governing AI with purpose',short:'Safeguards are a system',space:'The governance office',question:'What policies and safeguards are necessary for responsible implementation?',
lead:'Approval is the beginning of accountability, not the end of scrutiny.',
definition:'AI governance is the system of policies, procedures, responsibilities, and review mechanisms that directs and constrains organizational AI use.',
analysis:[
'Tool review begins with educational purpose and procurement, then examines what information is collected, where it goes, retention and deletion, security, vendor reuse, accessibility, bias, and human oversight. Assign responsibility for approval and ongoing review. A marketing promise is not a verified safeguard, and a product’s accessible interface does not guarantee that its generated materials are accessible.',
'Legal compliance and ethical responsibility overlap but are not identical. FERPA concerns education records at covered institutions; COPPA concerns covered online operators and children under 13. School-context consent is not unrestricted permission for commercial reuse. Section 504, IDEA, and applicable disability protections also require institutional attention. Specific applicability and obligations must be determined by qualified privacy, legal, and accessibility staff; this module is not a legal determination.',
'Operational governance includes student-use expectations, academic integrity, transparency about assistance, appeal routes, incident response, and policy revision. A suspected data exposure needs containment, the responsible staff contact, documented facts, and applicable notification procedures—not an informal promise to be more careful. Define a review date and triggers such as a vendor change, new age group, or accessibility complaint.'
],
elementary:'Before a literacy pilot with young learners, the school reviews age eligibility, operator practices, allowable data, accessible alternatives, and family communication. Teachers test with synthetic examples while procurement questions remain open.',
secondary:'A school authorizes a bounded writing-support pilot with clear AI-use disclosure, human review, and no automated final grades. A student can request review of a biased response, and the school records the issue for vendor follow-up.',
inclusion:'Require specialist and learner testing with assistive technologies. Check generated documents, keyboard operation, language access, and reasonable alternatives. Vendor assurances alone cannot replace testing the actual workflow.',
limits:'A checklist cannot prove security or legal compliance. A tool can pass a review and later change its terms or behavior. Governance needs resourced monitoring and a genuine ability to suspend use.',
strategies:['Assign accountable owners for procurement, instruction, privacy, accessibility, and incidents.','Require evidence for vendor claims and specify data minimization.','Publish allowed uses, assistance boundaries, appeals, and review triggers.'],
reflection:'Who could stop an unsafe AI practice tomorrow? Is that responsibility documented and understood?',
synthesis:'Governance turns good intentions into responsibilities that can be checked, challenged, and revised.',sources:['brief','ferpa','coppa','ada'],activity:'governance',model:['Purpose','Privacy','Accessibility','Oversight','Review']
},
{
id:7,act:2,title:'Ethical and inclusive leadership',short:'Design for belonging',space:'The inclusive classroom',question:'How can leaders ensure AI expands opportunity rather than reinforcing inequity?',
lead:'An innovation is incomplete if its benefits depend on which learner enters the room.',
definition:'Inclusive leadership deliberately involves affected people and removes barriers so learners can participate, exercise agency, and pursue meaningful goals.',
analysis:[
'Equality provides the same resource; equity responds to different barriers and needs. Accessibility makes information and interaction usable, including with assistive technology. Inclusion concerns participation, belonging, and influence over decisions. These ideas intersect, but equal device distribution alone cannot establish any of the others.',
'Universal Design for Learning invites planning for variability through multiple means of engagement, representation, and action/expression. Start with a clear learning goal and separate it from unnecessary demands of one format. AI may support reading, translation, communication, or differentiated feedback, but it can also introduce factual errors, stereotyped examples, inaccessible formatting, or simplified work that reduces intellectual demand.',
'Inclusive implementation plans for device access, connectivity, cultural and linguistic relevance, assistive technology, and learner choice before a launch. Qualified educators and specialists must evaluate AI-suggested accommodations in context; an automated suggestion does not replace an individualized plan or professional responsibility. Invite learners to identify barriers and provide an equivalent route without requiring personal disclosure.'
],
elementary:'A science lesson offers verified text with vocabulary support, captions for a demonstration, and tactile materials. Learners explain plant growth by drawing, speech, or accessible writing while all responses must use the same scientific ideas.',
secondary:'Students evaluate a historical argument using source excerpts available in accessible formats. Vocabulary supports and reviewed translations aid access without replacing analysis. A learner can record an explanation or write one using the same evidence rubric.',
inclusion:'Check language meaning with a qualified speaker when it matters; check reading supports for accuracy; test keyboard and screen-reader access. Consult IEP/504 teams for individual accommodations. Avoid using cultural background or disability labels as proxies for ability.',
limits:'More formats can create workload and inconsistent quality. A modality choice must still provide evidence of the targeted construct. AI is not an interpreter, disability specialist, or unbiased decision-maker by default.',
strategies:['Keep the learning goal stable while offering meaningful routes to it.','Test barriers with learners and specialists before rollout.','Monitor who benefits, who opts out, and who still lacks access.'],
reflection:'Which demand in your current lesson measures a format skill rather than the intended learning? How could you remove that barrier?',
synthesis:'Inclusive design protects rigor by expanding access to the learning goal.',sources:['brief','udl','ada'],activity:'inclusive',model:['Engagement','Representation','Action & expression','Agency']
},
{
id:8,act:2,title:'Engaging the school community',short:'Trust is a conversation',space:'The community meeting space',question:'How do leaders build community trust around AI implementation?',
lead:'Transparency means making the decision understandable—and making participation consequential.',
definition:'Stakeholder engagement is a sustained process in which affected people help define concerns, shape decisions, and evaluate their consequences.',
analysis:[
'Informing a community after procurement is communication, but it is not meaningful participation in the decision. Students, families, teachers, administrators, coaches, specialists, and community partners hold different knowledge. A useful engagement plan defines what they can influence, how input is gathered, and how leaders will respond. Listening sessions need feedback loops rather than a silent collection of comments.',
'Address concrete questions: What task will the tool support? What data will be used? Who checks misinformation? What happens when a learner cannot access it? How will screen time, dependence, fairness, and academic integrity be examined? Avoid either dismissing concerns or promising perfect safety. A credible response separates known safeguards, unresolved questions, and the next action.',
'Elementary engagement often requires careful family explanation, age-appropriate student participation, and attention to young learners’ privacy. Secondary students can help design assistance boundaries and critique assessment expectations, but they still need protective structures. Accessible notices, reviewed translation, flexible meeting options, and non-digital channels prevent the participation process from recreating the access problem.'
],
elementary:'A school shares a plain-language pilot description at a family meeting and on paper. Families ask about reading supports and data. Leaders explain that teachers use synthetic examples during review and publish answers to unresolved vendor questions before any student use.',
secondary:'Students co-design an AI-use disclosure form and discuss where independent reasoning is required. Teachers and families review the proposal, and the school explains which changes were accepted and which safeguards remain non-negotiable.',
inclusion:'Provide captions, accessible documents, interpretation where needed, and ways to contribute without attending a meeting. Include special and multilingual educators in communication planning. Do not ask people to disclose sensitive needs publicly.',
limits:'Participants are not a statistically representative sample. Vocal support can obscure absent voices. Consultation cannot override legal protections or delegate school responsibilities to families.',
strategies:['Publish purpose, scope, known risks, review owners, and a feedback route.','Seek input from people unlikely to attend a public forum.','Close the loop with a decision record and a date for follow-up.'],
reflection:'Whose voice is missing from your usual consultation? What route would let that person influence the plan?',
synthesis:'Trust depends on understandable commitments, honest uncertainty, and visible responses to participation.',sources:['brief'],activity:'community',model:['Students','Families','Educators','Specialists','Leadership']
},
{
id:9,act:2,title:'Redefining assessment in the AI era',short:'Quality is not the whole story',space:'The assessment design studio',question:'If AI can generate high-quality work, what constitutes meaningful evidence of student learning?',
lead:'A polished product tells us something about the product. It does not, by itself, tell us what the learner understands.',
definition:'Authentic assessment asks learners to apply knowledge and judgment to a meaningful task, with evidence that makes their reasoning and decisions visible.',
analysis:[
'AI challenges the assumption that a fluent essay or correct answer demonstrates independent understanding. Distinguish quality of the artifact from evidence of the learner’s disciplinary knowledge. Formative checkpoints reveal emerging thinking; performance tasks reveal application; portfolios show development across time. None is automatically authentic merely because it has a new label.',
'Redesign the evidence rather than simply ban or detect a tool. Ask for a proposal, a justified source choice, annotated revisions, an explanation of an error, and a final performance connected to the goal. Rubrics can value reasoning, evidence, disciplinary accuracy, creativity where relevant, and reflection. Publish explicit expectations for allowed assistance, required disclosure, and independently demonstrated skills. Students need to understand both the rule and its educational purpose.',
'Process documentation and a brief oral explanation can support interpretation, but they must be accessible and proportionate. AI detection alone should not decide misconduct: investigate work and context, hear the student, and follow a fair institutional process. This module offers original questions for the assigned Bowen and Watson Chapter 10 on grading and quality. The chapter itself was not accessed, so these are not attributed chapter findings.'
],
elementary:'Instead of grading only a polished habitat poster, a teacher observes students sorting evidence, asks them to explain an animal adaptation, and collects a revised drawing. Approved text supports aid expression while reasoning remains visible.',
secondary:'Students write a local environmental recommendation with annotated sources, a draft conference, documented AI assistance if permitted, and an accessible explanation of why their evidence supports the recommendation. The rubric separates argument quality from formatting polish.',
inclusion:'Offer accessible process evidence and alternatives to a live oral defense where needed. Avoid penalizing speech, typing, or English fluency when those are not the construct. Specialists help preserve rigor while honoring accommodations and language development.',
limits:'Checkpoints increase review workload and can become surveillance if excessive. A reflection can itself be generated. Triangulate proportionate evidence rather than claim that any single method proves authorship or understanding.',
strategies:['Name the construct and the skill students must demonstrate independently.','Add a small number of meaningful process checkpoints and performance evidence.','Publish assistance rules, disclosure, an accessible rubric, and a fair review route.'],
reflection:'What evidence would persuade you that a student understands a claim even if AI helped express it?',
synthesis:'Assessment quality comes from a defensible interpretation of learning evidence, not just the appearance of a final product.',sources:['brief','bowen'],activity:'assessment',model:['Product','Process','Reasoning','Performance','Reflection']
},
{
id:10,act:3,title:'Choosing responsible innovation',short:'The best answer may be non-AI',space:'The innovation laboratory',question:'When should schools use AI, and when should they choose another approach?',
lead:'Responsible innovation begins with a problem worth solving and remains willing to choose a simpler solution.',
definition:'Responsible innovation is the deliberate evaluation and adaptation of a proposed change in light of educational value, feasibility, ethics, and long-term consequences.',
analysis:[
'The newest tool is not necessarily the most suitable intervention. Define the educational need before selecting a product, then compare AI and non-AI approaches. A lack of timely feedback may reflect class size, unclear rubrics, or scheduling rather than a need for automation. Cost includes licenses, review time, coaching, accessibility work, support, and exit arrangements.',
'A responsible decision process identifies the need, considers alternatives, evaluates benefits and risks, examines equity and accessibility, defines success indicators, pilots carefully, gathers evidence, and then refines, scales, or discontinues. Scaling is one possible outcome, not the predetermined reward for completing a pilot. A decision record should show what assumptions remain uncertain and what would trigger a stop.',
'Sustainability means that the practice can survive staff turnover, vendor changes, and budget constraints without making learners dependent on one product. Retain exportable work, a viable alternative, and human expertise. Bounded innovation protects the ability to learn from a trial; it does not justify exposing learners to avoidable risk simply because something is experimental.'
],
elementary:'A school considers automated reading assignments but discovers that students lack access to enough suitable books. A library partnership and guided reading plan address the immediate barrier more directly, while the AI proposal remains a later question.',
secondary:'A department compares AI feedback, peer review with a shared rubric, and additional teacher conferencing. It pilots a narrow feedback task only after review and measures usefulness, teacher checking time, and students’ independent revisions.',
inclusion:'Budget for accessible alternatives and specialist review at the start. Evaluate whether benefits depend on home connectivity, premium accounts, or English-only output. A cheaper product can be more costly if it excludes learners.',
limits:'A low-risk option may still have opportunity costs. Pilots involve selection effects and short time horizons. Avoid treating a promising local example as evidence that every department should adopt the same approach.',
strategies:['Compare at least one viable non-AI solution.','Calculate review, support, accessibility, and exit costs alongside price.','Set explicit refine, scale, and stop criteria before the pilot.'],
reflection:'Which educational problem in your setting might be solved better through relationships, time, or curriculum than through AI?',
synthesis:'Responsible innovation values an appropriate solution more than adoption of a particular technology.',sources:['brief'],activity:'innovation',model:['Need','Alternatives','Review','Pilot','Refine / stop']
},
{
id:11,act:3,title:'Learning through continuous improvement',short:'Evidence before expansion',space:'The evaluation center',question:'How can leaders determine whether AI implementation is improving education?',
lead:'High adoption can coexist with weak learning. A leader asks what changed, for whom, and at what cost.',
definition:'Continuous improvement is disciplined, repeated inquiry that uses evidence to refine practice toward an explicit aim.',
analysis:[
'Adoption rates describe use; they do not establish effectiveness. Examine student understanding, feedback quality, teacher workload, engagement, accessibility, equity, student agency, educator confidence, stakeholder trust, and implementation quality. Pair quantitative indicators with work samples, interviews, observations, and incident reports. Define each measure so a change can be interpreted rather than merely displayed.',
'Baseline evidence clarifies the starting point. Comparisons help identify changes, but an observational pilot is vulnerable to differences in students, staffing, tasks, and timing. A gain after implementation is not proof that AI caused it. Disaggregate where appropriate without exposing small groups, and look for unintended consequences such as increased checking time or dependence on generated answers.',
'A Plan–Do–Study–Act cycle makes uncertainty productive. Plan a bounded change and a prediction; do it on a limited scale; study the evidence against the prediction; act by adapting, testing again, expanding cautiously, or stopping. Record what actually happened, including incomplete implementation. An elegant dashboard is useful only if it leads to a better question and a responsible next decision.'
],
elementary:'A literacy team samples explanations before and during a question-design pilot. It records review time and asks students whether supports helped them participate. Mixed results lead to revising questions rather than expanding immediately.',
secondary:'A writing pilot has strong usage but little change in independent reasoning. Students report helpful editing yet weak feedback on evidence. Leaders narrow the use to editing support and redesign the argument-feedback routine for another cycle.',
inclusion:'Compare access and experience across learner groups with privacy-protective aggregation. Include qualitative reports of assistive-technology barriers; a small number of serious exclusions matters even when the average score looks favorable.',
limits:'Small fictional dashboards in this seminar illustrate interpretation, not real findings. Real pilots need clear denominators, consistent tasks, and documented uncertainty. Neither a single score nor a before/after difference proves causation.',
strategies:['Collect baseline learning evidence and total teacher workload.','Triangulate measures and examine distribution of benefits and barriers.','Use a review meeting to choose a specific next test, not just report activity.'],
reflection:'What evidence would lead you to discontinue a popular AI initiative?',
synthesis:'Improvement means testing assumptions and responding to evidence, including evidence against the initiative.',sources:['brief','pdsa'],activity:'evidence',model:['Plan','Do','Study','Act']
},
{
id:12,act:3,title:'National and global leadership perspectives',short:'Read guidance with a purpose',space:'The policy comparison studio',question:'What principles should guide AI leadership across educational systems?',
lead:'A local decision benefits from both instructional questions and a wider view of rights, inclusion, and accountability.',
definition:'A policy reading lens is a structured set of questions used to connect professional guidance to a local decision without confusing recommendations with law.',
analysis:[
'The supplied brief assigns the U.S. Department of Education report and UNESCO guidance as complementary readings. It directs attention to human-centered learning, educator expertise, oversight, evaluation, inclusion, privacy, transparency, and accountability. Here these themes become questions for reading and local analysis. The U.S. report was retrieved and relevant recommendations were reviewed. The UNESCO record was reachable, but its full publication text could not be retrieved. UNESCO discussion remains a course-brief reading lens rather than a verified textual summary.',
'The U.S. report’s Recommendation 1 emphasizes humans in the loop (printed pp. 53–54). Recommendation 2 aligns AI to a shared educational vision and identifies privacy, notice and explanation, protection from discrimination, safety and effectiveness, and human alternatives (pp. 54–56). Recommendation 3 emphasizes modern learning principles and intentional inclusion (p. 56). Use UNESCO as a complementary reading lens for rights, inclusion, ethics, and accountability across contexts, as directed by the supplied brief. These themes overlap rather than divide neatly by publication.',
'Professional recommendations can inform governance but do not become legally binding merely because an official organization publishes them. Local law, institution type, age group, and procurement conditions require separate review. A practical comparison records the dilemma, the relevant passage and page in each source, a proposed local action, and an unresolved question. The national-source summary is traceable to reviewed pages; the global-source side remains a discussion scaffold until the publication is checked.'
],
elementary:'For a translation-support proposal, a team uses the two reading lenses to ask who checks instructional meaning, how families understand the use, and whether language access and privacy protections are adequate.',
secondary:'For an AI feedback pilot, students and educators ask how human oversight, learner agency, transparent assistance rules, and evaluation should appear in the plan. They locate supporting passages before citing either publication in policy.',
inclusion:'A global perspective can expose assumptions about connectivity, language, culture, and age. A national perspective can support local instructional evaluation. Neither should erase the voices of learners and families affected by the decision.',
limits:'The U.S. report’s cited recommendations were verified, but a full two-document textual comparison is not claimed. Review UNESCO and record supporting passages before attributing source-specific global claims. Guidance must be adapted to context and distinguished from applicable legal duties.',
strategies:['Read the actual sources and record passages with page numbers.','Compare common principles and contextual differences without caricature.','Translate a principle into an owner, procedure, indicator, and review date.'],
reflection:'Which principle is already present in your policy but absent in everyday practice? What would operationalizing it look like?',
synthesis:'Guidance is useful when it sharpens a decision and remains traceable to its actual source.',sources:['brief','ed','unesco'],activity:'policy',model:['Instructional purpose','Human oversight','Rights & inclusion','Accountability']
},
{
id:13,act:3,title:'Preparing future-ready learners',short:'Preserve the power to think',space:'The future-ready school',question:'What should students still learn to do independently when AI can produce an answer?',
lead:'The ability to obtain an answer matters. The ability to judge, explain, and act on it matters more.',
definition:'Future readiness combines disciplinary knowledge, human capabilities, AI literacy, ethical judgment, and learner agency for navigating changing conditions.',
analysis:[
'Academic knowledge remains essential because evaluating an output requires knowing what makes it plausible, accurate, and relevant. Critical thinking, creativity, collaboration, and communication are practices grounded in subject matter, not detachable slogans. AI literacy includes understanding limitations, inspecting outputs, and recognizing when assistance is inappropriate.',
'Productive struggle can help learners build durable understanding; unnecessary barriers can prevent it. The leader’s task is to distinguish support that opens access from assistance that performs the intended thinking. A student might use speech-to-text while independently constructing an argument. Another might need to attempt a solution before asking a tool for critique. The learning goal determines the boundary.',
'Ethical reasoning and agency mean learners can question a recommendation, disclose assistance, consider effects on others, and choose a different route. Educators increasingly design those conditions and model disciplined inquiry. This is preparation for uncertainty, not a prediction about which jobs will disappear or how capable future systems will be. Teach adaptable judgment without abandoning foundational knowledge.'
],
elementary:'Students investigate whether a generated animal fact fits what they learned from a trusted source. They explain their check and practice asking for help when an answer is confusing rather than accepting fluent language as proof.',
secondary:'Students compare an AI-generated civic argument with primary evidence, identify omissions, and build their own justified recommendation. Approved language supports aid communication, but students must defend the reasoning independently.',
inclusion:'Agency requires accessible choices. Assistive technology should not be mistaken for unearned help. Define independent thinking separately from independent physical production, and maintain the supports specified for the learner.',
limits:'Independence is context-dependent. A rigid “no support” rule can exclude learners, while unrestricted generation can bypass the target skill. Avoid unsupported forecasts about technological capabilities or employment.',
strategies:['Specify the thinking learners must own and the support they may use.','Teach critique, disclosure, and appropriate non-use alongside prompt skills.','Design tasks where knowledge, collaboration, and ethical reasoning interact.'],
reflection:'Which intellectual responsibility should remain with the learner in your next AI-supported task?',
synthesis:'Future readiness is the capacity to learn and judge with tools while retaining human agency.',sources:['brief'],activity:'future',model:['Academic knowledge','Human capabilities','AI literacy','Ethical judgment']
},
{
id:14,act:3,title:'Schoolwide AI leadership simulation',short:'Lead through the tradeoffs',space:'The district leadership room',question:'Can you guide a school through responsible AI implementation?',
lead:'You have a constrained budget, uneven readiness, and a community asking good questions. Every decision creates the next set of conditions.',
definition:'An implementation plan is a coordinated set of actions, owners, resources, safeguards, evidence, and review points for testing and sustaining a change.',
analysis:[
'In this fictional district, a proposed AI rollout spans instruction, assessment, and professional practice. The budget cannot support every initiative. Educators differ in experience; families have privacy and fairness concerns; leaders want visible progress. You will make decisions about scope, procurement, access, learning, communication, assessment, and evaluation.',
'The simulation tracks trust, instructional alignment, equity, readiness, and risk management as illustrative indicators. They are not probabilities, real district outcomes, or validated measures of leadership. Tradeoffs are deliberate: a fast launch can produce visible activity while leaving unresolved safeguards; a careful pilot takes time and may require explaining a slower timetable.',
'A sustainable plan states what will happen next and who will do it. It needs a budget for review and coaching, explicit assistance expectations, an accessible alternative, an incident route, baseline evidence, and criteria for refining, scaling, or stopping. Reflect on your decisions as a coherent pattern rather than chase a score. The resulting profile is a teaching aid for identifying strengths and improvement opportunities.'
],
elementary:'An elementary pilot first limits use to teacher planning with synthetic examples while reviewing age eligibility and family communication. Reading specialists and accessibility staff test outputs before any student-facing workflow.',
secondary:'A secondary pilot focuses on feedback for a defined writing task. The plan includes assistance disclosure, accessible process evidence, teacher review, student voice, and a scheduled evaluation of independent reasoning.',
inclusion:'A rollout must budget for assistive-technology review, language access, devices, and equivalent alternatives. Participation in a pilot cannot require learners to surrender privacy or waive accommodations.',
limits:'The simplified branches omit many real-world constraints. No profile diagnoses a personality or certifies leadership competence. Real implementation requires local consultation, legal review, and sustained evidence.',
strategies:['Allocate owners and time for every safeguard, not just purchases.','Keep a risk register and an actionable stop route.','Use the profile to revise one weak link in your plan.'],
reflection:'Which tradeoff in the simulation resembles a decision you face, and what additional evidence would you need?',
synthesis:'Responsible implementation is a sequence of connected decisions with visible consequences and room to revise.',sources:['brief'],activity:'simulation',model:['Trust','Alignment','Equity','Readiness','Risk management']
},
{
id:15,act:3,title:'The W.I.L.M.U. leadership capstone',short:'Turn a framework into a commitment',space:'The leadership capstone environment',question:'How does AI literacy become responsible educational leadership?',
lead:'The framework is a way of thinking across decisions—not five boxes to check and forget.',
definition:'A leadership philosophy is an articulated set of values and commitments that explains how a leader will judge decisions, act responsibly, and learn from consequences.',
analysis:[
'W — What Is AI? grounds decisions in capabilities, limitations, and an educational need. I — Investigate Outputs connects that understanding to evidence, bias, credibility, reliability, and suitability. L — Lead Responsibly turns evaluation into ethical action through privacy, governance, transparency, and professional judgment.',
'M — Maximize Learning asks whether the resulting practice is meaningful, inclusive, engaging, and capable of revealing authentic understanding. U — Understand the Future develops agency and adaptability without treating human-centered education as a temporary constraint. Each domain changes the others: an accessibility barrier is also an evaluation issue, a governance issue, and a learning issue.',
'A manifesto becomes useful when it names an action and a way to review it. Connect values to responsibilities toward learners, ethical boundaries, equity, professional growth, and human judgment. Then identify one next step, a partner, and evidence you will seek. Your responses remain in this browser; export them if you want a durable copy. Do not enter student names, records, or other personal information.'
],
elementary:'A teacher leader commits to verifying reading supports with colleagues, offering accessible alternatives, and gathering evidence of comprehension before recommending expansion. The commitment links all five domains to a specific instructional decision.',
secondary:'A department leader commits to transparent AI assistance rules, accessible reasoning checkpoints, student consultation, and periodic review of feedback usefulness. The plan preserves both disciplinary rigor and learner agency.',
inclusion:'A manifesto should state whose participation might be overlooked and how the leader will remove barriers. A commitment to equity is incomplete without a concrete access check and a route for concerns to influence decisions.',
limits:'A written philosophy is not evidence that its commitments are practiced. Revisit the manifesto with colleagues and compare it with decisions, learner experience, and implementation evidence.',
strategies:['Connect each domain to one real professional responsibility.','Write a feasible next action, partner, indicator, and review date.','Use reflection and evidence to revise the commitment over time.'],
reflection:'What will you protect, what will you change, and how will you know your leadership is strengthening learning?',
synthesis:'Technology may change how we teach. It should never change why we teach.',sources:['brief'],activity:'manifesto',model:['What Is AI?','Investigate Outputs','Lead Responsibly','Maximize Learning','Understand the Future']
}
];
